          "type": "html",
          "html": "<div class=\"kicker\">02</div>\n<h2>What this is</h2>\n<p class=\"lead\">This is the Dyad.</p>\n<p>You can use it with another person, or you can use it on your own.</p>\n<p>With another person, one of you speaks and the other listens within a very particular structure.</p>\n<p>On your own, I call it mind clearing. Same prompt. Same timer. Same principle of staying with the question rather than taking your first answer and moving on.</p>\n<p>I have run the Dyad with 650 couples and thousands of clients, and taught it to hundreds of therapists.</p>\n<p class=\"big\">And it is almost <em>insultingly simple.</em></p>\n<div class=\"loop\"><span>One prompt.</span><span class=\"ar\">·</span><span>Fifteen minutes.</span><span class=\"ar\">·</span><span>Three possible responses from the listener.</span></div>\n<p>That is the structure.</p>\n<p>In The Three Answers, you're going to practise it across seven sittings, with one new prompt opening each day.</p>\n<h3>Why the timer matters</h3>\n<p>Fifteen minutes gives the conversation edges.</p>\n<p>You both know when you are beginning, and you both know there is an ending.</p>\n<p>Nobody has to wonder: How long is this going to go on?</p>\n<p>You have agreed to stay with one prompt for fifteen minutes. That is the container.</p>\n<p>Because you are not rushing to finish the conversation or move on to the next question, you get to stay with the same thing for longer than you normally might.</p>\n<p>The first answer may come quickly. Then the same prompt comes back to you. You answer again. Again.</p>\n<p>You may discover that the answer you had ready was not the only answer you had.</p>\n<p>That is why the timer matters.</p>\n<p>Not because fifteen minutes is a magic number.</p>\n<p class=\"big\">The fifteen minutes gives you <em>somewhere to stay.</em></p>"
