          "html": "<p class=\"quiet\" style=\"text-align:center;font-size:15px\">Tap the link once and it's yours on that phone. Add it to your home screen and you can come straight back to it each day.</p>\n<p>Your first prompt will be waiting for you. The next one opens the following morning.</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">That is deliberate.</p>\n<p>You are not supposed to see all seven at once, choose your favourite or race ahead.</p>\n<p>Each prompt gets its own fifteen-minute sitting.</p>\n<p>The prompts are in an order. They begin more simply and gradually ask a little more of you as you become familiar with the Dyad.</p>\n<p>Don't skip ahead. Don't double up.</p>\n<p>If you can, do one sitting a day for seven days. If you miss a day, that's fine. Pick it up again the next day.</p>\n<p class=\"big\">One prompt. One sitting. <em>Fifteen minutes.</em></p>\n<div class=\"rolecard\" style=\"margin-top:40px\">\n<span class=\"tag\">On your own</span>\n<h3>You can also do this on your own.</h3>\n<p>I call this mind clearing, and it isn't the consolation version.</p>\n<p>The principle is the same: one prompt, a clear container, and staying with the question after you think you've answered it.</p>\n<ol class=\"qs\">\n<li>Choose the 10-minute timer in the app.</li>\n<li>Read the prompt out loud.</li>\n<li>Answer it out loud to the room or write your answer down, whichever you will actually do.</li>\n<li>When you run out of things to say, read the same prompt again. Answer it again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Keep going until the timer goes.</p>\n<p>That moment when you think, I've got nothing else, is not a reason to stop. You may simply have reached the end of the answer that came most easily.</p>\n<p>Ask the question again and see what else is there.</p>\n<p class=\"quiet\">There is no Clarify That or Summarise That when you're on your own. The repetition does that job.</p>\n<p class=\"big\" style=\"margin:16px 0 0\">Same question. Again. <em>Until the timer goes.</em></p>\n<h3>A note on two of the prompts</h3>\n<p>Two of your seven prompts are written specifically for a partner. You'll recognise them because they use words like \"by me\" or \"we.\"</p>\n<p>If you're doing The Three Answers on your own, take the partner language out and ask the underlying question of yourself.</p>\n<p class=\"quiet\" style=\"margin:0\">The other five can be used exactly as written.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-top:30px\">Your seven sittings</p>"
